   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend a chapter of the laws of 2022, directing the department of\nagriculture and markets, in cooperation with the New York state energy\nresearch and development authority, to develop and produce guidance and\neducational materials on the use of agrivoltaics in farming, relating to\ncooperative extension offices and New York state soil and water conservation\ndistricts",
    "basePrintNo": "S820",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S820-2023",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §2, Chap of 2022 (as proposed in S.7861-A & A.9336-A)",
    "lawSection": "Agriculture and Markets",
    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S820\n\nSPONSOR: MAY\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend a chapter of the laws of 2022, directing the department\nof agriculture and markets, in cooperation with the New York state ener-\ngy research and development authority, to develop and produce guidance\nand educational materials on the use of agrivoltaics in farming, relat-\ning to cooperative extension offices and New York state soil and water\nconservation districts\n\n \nPURPOSE OF BILL:\n\nThis is a chapter amendment that makes changes to provisions of L.2022,\nc.629.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS:\n\nThis legislation extends the timeframe for enactment of the bill's\nprovisions from 120 days after the effective date to one year.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nPursuant to approval memo 27 of 2022, the Executive supports this legis-\nlation, as it will promote climate resilience and reduce energy costs\nfor farmers, but the state needs one year to develop guidance and educa-\ntional materials on agrivoltaics.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nChapter amendment to Chapter 629 of the Laws of 2022.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS FOR STATE AND LOCAL GOVERNMENTS:\n\nNone.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect on the same date and in the same manner as a\nchapter of the laws of 2022 directing the department of agriculture and\nmarkets, in cooperation with the New York state energy research and\ndevelopment authority, to develop and produce guidance and educational\nmaterials on the use of agrivoltaics in farming, as proposed in legisla-\ntive bills numbers S. 7861-A and A. 9336-A, takes effect.\n",
